## Conventions

- **Filenames:** kebab-case, e.g. `transformer-architecture.md`, `andrej-karpathy.md`.
- **Wikilinks:** prefer `[[page-slug]]` over markdown links for internal references.
- **Wikilink pipes:** never escape `|` inside `[[…]]`. Use `[[slug|alias]]`, not `[[slug\|alias]]` — even inside markdown tables. The lint scanner captures the slash as part of the slug and the link silently breaks.
- **Dates:** ISO format `YYYY-MM-DD` everywhere.
- **Frontmatter:** required on every wiki page (see below).
- **Cross-references:** every page must link to at least 2 other pages.
- **Provenance:** on pages synthesizing 3+ sources, append `^[raw/articles/source.md]`
  at the end of paragraphs whose claims come from a specific source. Optional on
  single-source pages where the `sources:` frontmatter is sufficient.

## Frontmatter (required on all Layer 2 pages)

```yaml
---
title: Page Title
created: YYYY-MM-DD
updated: YYYY-MM-DD
type: entity | concept | comparison | query | summary
tags: [from-taxonomy-only]
sources: [raw/articles/source-name.md]
# Optional quality signals:
confidence: high | medium | low        # how well-supported the claims are
contested: true                        # set when there are unresolved contradictions
contradictions: [other-page-slug]      # pages this one conflicts with
---
```

`confidence` and `contested` are recommended for opinion-heavy or fast-moving
topics. The lint script surfaces `contested: true` and `confidence: low` pages
for review so weak claims don't silently harden into accepted wiki fact.

`sources:` must list every `raw/` file whose content informed the page. On
pages that synthesize across multiple raw articles (cross-cut entities,
multi-source concepts and comparisons), list all of them — not just the
primary one. The lint script flags missing or empty `sources:`.

## Frontmatter for raw/ sources

```yaml
---
source_url: https://example.com/article   # original URL, if applicable
ingested: YYYY-MM-DD
sha256: <hex digest of body, computed below the closing --->
---
```

Compute `sha256` with the bundled helper — it matches `lint.py`'s drift
check exactly:

```bash
python3 "$HOME/.claude/skills/wikify/scripts/compute_body_sha256.py" raw/articles/source-name.md
```

The helper hashes everything after the closing `---\n` of the frontmatter
block (UTF-8 bytes, including any trailing newline at EOF). On re-ingest
of the same URL, recompute and compare; skip if identical, flag drift if
different.

